1
00:00:00,316 --> 00:00:02,283
Have you ever shrunk a browser window

2
00:00:02,283 --> 00:00:04,133
and watched the text reflow?

3
00:00:04,316 --> 00:00:04,733
It seems so simple, so automatic.

4
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:09,983
A word just moves down to the next line.

5
00:00:10,366 --> 00:00:13,200
But what happens when the text isn't English?

6
00:00:13,633 --> 00:00:17,133
What happens when there are no spaces between words?

7
00:00:17,833 --> 00:00:20,533
Or when the language is written from right to left?

8
00:00:20,883 --> 00:00:23,100
That simple, automatic process

9
00:00:23,166 --> 00:00:25,633
suddenly becomes one of the most fascinating

10
00:00:25,633 --> 00:00:28,816
and complex challenges in web typography.

11
00:00:29,516 --> 00:00:31,733
Hello, I’m Fuqiao Xue,

12
00:00:32,033 --> 00:00:34,433
and I lead the Worldwide Web Consortium's

13
00:00:34,433 --> 00:00:36,466
Internationalization Activity.

14
00:00:36,833 --> 00:00:39,600
Our job is to ensure that web technologies

15
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:42,700
work for everyone, regardless of their language,

16
00:00:42,966 --> 00:00:44,733
script, or culture.

17
00:00:45,116 --> 00:00:46,033
Today, we’re taking

18
00:00:46,033 --> 00:00:48,283
a high-level look at the diverse strategies

19
00:00:48,283 --> 00:00:50,783
for breaking text at the end of a line.

20
00:00:51,233 --> 00:00:54,733
At its core, line breaking depends on two factors:

21
00:00:55,083 --> 00:00:55,983
first,

22
00:00:56,200 --> 00:00:59,400
whether words or syllables are separated in the text,

23
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:01,733
and how. And second,

24
00:01:01,833 --> 00:01:04,216
whether the system wraps whole words,

25
00:01:04,316 --> 00:01:07,983
syllables, or individual characters to the next line.

26
00:01:08,633 --> 00:01:12,616
Let's start with the familiar: wrapping by words.

27
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:17,616
Most European languages use spaces to separate words.

28
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:20,083
When a line gets too long,

29
00:01:20,083 --> 00:01:22,166
the browser looks for the last space

30
00:01:22,166 --> 00:01:24,216
and moves the next word down.

31
00:01:24,883 --> 00:01:27,566
This same principle applies to many other scripts,

32
00:01:27,566 --> 00:01:31,416
like Devanagari, used for Hindi and some languages, and Arabic.

33
00:01:31,916 --> 00:01:33,800
Of course, with Arabic,

34
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:35,933
the text flows from right to left.

35
00:01:36,166 --> 00:01:40,183
So when it wraps, the new line still starts on the right.

36
00:01:40,766 --> 00:01:42,933
But what if there are no spaces?

37
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:44,933
Scripts like Thai,

38
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,700
Lao, and Khmer are written without separating words.

39
00:01:49,150 --> 00:01:52,250
Here, browsers can't just look for a space.

40
00:01:52,550 --> 00:01:56,516
They need a dictionary. The browser analyzes the text

41
00:01:56,516 --> 00:02:00,533
and uses linguistic rules to identify word boundaries.

42
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:04,966
As you can imagine, it’s not always 100% perfect.

43
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:06,233
Sometimes,

44
00:02:06,233 --> 00:02:09,616
the definition of a 'word' itself can be subjective.

45
00:02:09,866 --> 00:02:11,200
Let's move on.

46
00:02:11,583 --> 00:02:14,666
Some writing systems don't wrap by the word,

47
00:02:14,666 --> 00:02:16,116
but by the syllable.

48
00:02:16,566 --> 00:02:18,516
Tibetan is a great example.

49
00:02:18,533 --> 00:02:21,316
Each syllable is followed by a small mark.

50
00:02:21,566 --> 00:02:25,433
Line breaking in Tibetan happens right after this mark.

51
00:02:25,933 --> 00:02:28,716
It's preferable to keep a whole word together,

52
00:02:28,816 --> 00:02:32,400
but breaking at any syllable is perfectly acceptable.

53
00:02:33,133 --> 00:02:36,266
Modern Korean text uses spaces between words,

54
00:02:36,333 --> 00:02:39,550
but authors can choose to wrap either by the word,

55
00:02:39,616 --> 00:02:43,800
like English, or by the syllable, which is each character.

56
00:02:44,616 --> 00:02:45,866
Syllable-based wrapping

57
00:02:45,866 --> 00:02:48,916
can be very useful for creating justified text,

58
00:02:49,216 --> 00:02:52,083
which is common in East Asian typography.

59
00:02:52,416 --> 00:02:56,283
And finally, we have systems that read by the character.

60
00:02:56,933 --> 00:02:59,366
Japanese is a well known example.

61
00:02:59,733 --> 00:03:00,666
In Japanese,

62
00:03:00,666 --> 00:03:03,883
lines can break between almost any two characters,

63
00:03:03,983 --> 00:03:06,683
regardless of word or syllable boundaries.

64
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:09,400
This gives a lot of flexibility,

65
00:03:09,583 --> 00:03:13,166
especially in narrow columns like in newspapers.

66
00:03:13,583 --> 00:03:16,483
However, there are still complex rules.

67
00:03:16,816 --> 00:03:20,183
For instance, you generally can't start a new line

68
00:03:20,183 --> 00:03:22,266
with a closing punctuation mark,

69
00:03:22,266 --> 00:03:25,283
like a period or a closing bracket.

70
00:03:25,750 --> 00:03:27,750
Beyond these broad categories

71
00:03:27,750 --> 00:03:29,850
lies a world of special rules.

72
00:03:30,316 --> 00:03:33,250
Hyphenation in German is different from English.

73
00:03:33,633 --> 00:03:35,600
Some scripts like, Ethiopic,

74
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:37,500
traditionally used a special wordspace

75
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:41,100
character, instead of a regular space character,

76
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:44,700
and it allows for breaking after any character.

77
00:03:45,083 --> 00:03:47,383
And there are even more dynamic rules.

78
00:03:47,566 --> 00:03:49,116
In traditional Tibetan,

79
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:50,450
if a line break

80
00:03:50,483 --> 00:03:53,483
leaves a single syllable before a punctuation mark

81
00:03:53,483 --> 00:03:54,583
called a 'shad'

82
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:56,183
that shad

83
00:03:56,350 --> 00:03:58,716
actually transforms to a different character

84
00:03:58,716 --> 00:03:59,800
on the new line

85
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:01,850
to indicate a continuation. And in Javanese,

86
00:04:02,500 --> 00:04:05,300
if a line breaks before a specific vowel sign,

87
00:04:05,300 --> 00:04:05,433
a 'ghost' of that sign is placed at the end of the previous line

88
00:04:07,783 --> 00:04:09,216
to aid readability.

89
00:04:09,216 --> 00:04:11,716
It's the same character in memory,

90
00:04:11,816 --> 00:04:13,200
but it's drawn twice on the screen.

91
00:04:15,416 --> 00:04:17,400
So,

92
00:04:18,616 --> 00:04:21,833
that simple line break isn't so simple after all.

93
00:04:22,300 --> 00:04:23,700
Browsers and standards

94
00:04:23,700 --> 00:04:25,933
have to account for all this diversity.

95
00:04:26,783 --> 00:04:30,716
This is why the work we do at the W3C is so vital.

96
00:04:31,100 --> 00:04:32,833
If you found this interesting

97
00:04:32,933 --> 00:04:36,366
you can read our full article on the W3C website,

98
00:04:36,466 --> 00:04:39,883
linked in the description below. Thanks for watching.

